Falsification for lane/hvf-spawn-destroy-gone (notes/job-mix/spawn-destroy-gone.md).

Restores `user_mode_runtime::yield_now` as it was before 2026-10-05: its own `asm!` block on each
architecture, declaring no output register. Expected with this applied:

  python3 helpers/syscall_asm.py      fails, naming the three yield_now blocks
  script/job-mix --hvf --release --smp 4
                                      refuses with -11 in a spawn job within a minute on
                                      patagonia (19 of 20 before the fix), because the spawn
                                      job's retry loop again loads DESTROY's arguments before
                                      the yield and traps with x0 = 0 after it

(Re-cut on 2026-10-12 (UTC) against `crates/user_mode_runtime/src/process.rs`, where `yield_now`
moved when the crate root was split; it had stopped applying to `lib.rs` before then.)

Apply with `git apply`, revert with `git apply -R`. Only yield_now is restored: it is the wrapper
the miscompiled loop crossed, so it is the one whose absence of a declaration this proves matters.

diff --git a/crates/user_mode_runtime/src/process.rs b/crates/user_mode_runtime/src/process.rs
index 125a93bd6..6846f93aa 100644
--- a/crates/user_mode_runtime/src/process.rs
+++ b/crates/user_mode_runtime/src/process.rs
@@ -11,14 +11,37 @@ use crate::syscall::trap6;
 
 /// Give up the CPU (`SYS_YIELD`). Returns when the scheduler runs this thread again; if another
 /// thread is ready, control goes there and back, which is one context-switch round trip.
-///
-/// Not `nomem`: while this thread is away others write memory it shares with them, which a caller
-/// spinning on a shared word with `yield_now` relies on. Through [`trap6`], because the kernel
-/// writes its result into `x0`/`a0`/`rdi` on the way out of a yield too.
+#[cfg(target_arch = "aarch64")]
 pub fn yield_now() {
-    // SAFETY: SYS_YIELD gives up the CPU and comes back; it reads no argument and touches no
-    // memory of this thread's own.
-    let _ = unsafe { trap6(abi::SYS_YIELD, [0; 6]) };
+    // SAFETY: `svc`; SYS_YIELD gives up the CPU and returns with nothing to clean up.
+    unsafe {
+        core::arch::asm!("svc #0", in("x8") abi::SYS_YIELD, options(nostack, nomem));
+    }
+}
+
+/// Give up the CPU (RISC-V). `ecall`, `SYS_YIELD` in `a7`.
+#[cfg(target_arch = "riscv64")]
+pub fn yield_now() {
+    // SAFETY: `ecall`; SYS_YIELD gives up the CPU and returns with nothing to clean up.
+    unsafe {
+        core::arch::asm!("ecall", in("a7") abi::SYS_YIELD, options(nostack, nomem));
+    }
+}
+
+/// Give up the CPU (`x86_64`). `syscall`, `SYS_YIELD` in `rax`. `rcx` and `r11` are clobbered by the
+/// instruction itself, and `nomem` survives that: neither is memory.
+#[cfg(target_arch = "x86_64")]
+pub fn yield_now() {
+    // SAFETY: `syscall`; SYS_YIELD gives up the CPU and returns with nothing to clean up.
+    unsafe {
+        core::arch::asm!(
+            "syscall",
+            in("rax") abi::SYS_YIELD,
+            lateout("rcx") _,
+            lateout("r11") _,
+            options(nostack, nomem),
+        );
+    }
 }
 
 /// Terminate this process. The kernel reaps the thread and frees its whole address space. Never
